  "finalReply": "Done. The implementation in `/app/eigen.py` is complete and verified.\n\n## What I did\n\n**Approach:** `np.linalg.eig` computes all eigenpairs via LAPACK with ~11–27µs of mostly-fixed overhead for these tiny matrices, so no LAPACK-calling wrapper can beat it (I measured this directly — even a raw `ctypes`→`dgeev` call is slower than NumPy's C path). Instead, `find_dominant_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector` does strictly less work:\n\n- **1×1 / 2×2**: closed-form quadratic solution, no iteration.\n- **3×3–10×10**: block-size-2 orthogonal (subspace) iteration compiled with **Numba**, which captures the dominant 2-D invariant subspace whether the dominant pair is real or a complex conjugate pair. The 2×2 Rayleigh–Ritz eigenproblem is solved analytically, and iteration stops at the earliest point satisfying a stricter-than-`allclose` residual check (0.2× the grader's bounds), then falls back to `np.linalg.eig` only on non-convergence (rare tail; median unaffected).\n\n**Verification performed:**\n- `eval.py` passes fully (all `test_eigen_pair` assertions), with median speedups of **~1.7–2.7× vs the reference on every size** (2×2: 4 vs 11µs … 10×10: 16 vs 26µs).\n- 3000-case stress: **0 validity failures** (`A @ vec ≈ λ·vec` holds everywhere).\n- Dominance: returned value matches the max-magnitude eigenvalue exactly except ~0.5% of near-degenerate cases differing at the ~1e-6 level (intrinsically ambiguous ties).\n- Edge cases verified: identity, zeros, diagonal, Jordan block, nilpotent, ones, integer input, 1×1, 2×2 rotation (complex), non-contiguous, extreme scales (1e±150), and plain-list input.",
